.TH GSP-KEYSTUDIO 1 "October 2026" "GSP-2026" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gsp-keystudio \- record and analyze audio with the Keyestudio ReSpeaker 2-Mic Pi HAT
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gsp-keystudio
.RB [ \-\-device
.IR DEVICE ]
.br
.B gsp-keystudio \-\-now
.RB [ \-\-device
.IR DEVICE ]
.br
.B gsp-keystudio \-\-file
.I FILE.wav
.br
.B gsp-keystudio \-\-play
.RI [ FILE.wav ]
.br
.B gsp-keystudio \-\-calibrate
.I DB
.RB [ \-\-file
.IR FILE.wav ]
.br
.B gsp-keystudio \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gsp-keystudio
turns a Raspberry Pi 3/4 with a Keyestudio ReSpeaker 2-Mic Pi HAT into a
button-operated recorder.
Tap the button on the HAT to record 3 seconds and get a frequency and
volume analysis, or double-tap it to record for as long as you like.
.PP
Recording uses the sound chip's highest rate, 48000 samples per second, so
it captures frequencies up to 24000 Hz.
Every recording is saved as a WAV file (16-bit, 48000 Hz, 2 channels; about
11.5 MB per minute) in
the recordings folder that the installer created, normally
.IR /home/pranav/recordings .
Every analysis report (button tap,
.B \-\-now
or
.BR \-\-file )
is also added to the end of
.IR /home/pranav/recordings/analysis_results.txt ,
with the date, time and the analyzed file, so you keep a history of all
your results.
.PP
With no options, the program waits for button taps until you press
.BR Ctrl+C .
.SH BUTTON CONTROLS
The button is the small push button built into the HAT (GPIO17).
.TP
.B Tap once
Records 3 seconds, prints the analysis report (see
.BR "THE ANALYSIS REPORT" ),
and saves the audio as
.IR rec_YYYYmmdd_HHMMSS_mmm.wav .
The recording starts about 0.4 seconds after you let go of the button,
because the program first checks whether a second tap is coming.
.TP
.B Double-tap
Starts a session recording with no time limit and no analysis.
The LEDs turn magenta and the program prints
.IR "Session recording started" .
.TP
.B Double-tap again
Stops the session and saves it as
.IR session_YYYYmmdd_HHMMSS_mmm.wav .
The program prints the file name and its length in seconds.
.TP
.B Single tap during a session
Ignored; the program prints a reminder to double-tap to stop.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Quits.
A session that is still recording is saved first.
.PP
Two taps count as a double-tap when the second press starts within
0.4 seconds of letting go of the first.
A quick third tap right after a double-tap is ignored.
Holding the button down does nothing until it is released.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-now
Record 3 seconds immediately, without waiting for the button, then
analyze and save it.
.TP
.BI \-\-file " FILE.wav"
Analyze an existing 16-bit WAV file.
Nothing is recorded or saved.
.TP
.BR \-\-play " [\fIFILE.wav\fR]"
Play a recording through the HAT's headphone jack.
With no file, plays the newest recording.
.I FILE.wav
can be a full path or just a file name from the recordings folder.
Press
.B Ctrl+C
to stop.
.TP
.BI \-\-calibrate " DB"
Make the volume readings match a sound level meter (a phone app works).
Hold the meter next to the HAT, make a steady sound (music, a fan, a
tone), and run
.B gsp-keystudio \-\-calibrate
with the meter's dB(A) reading; the program records 3 seconds and saves
the correction.
With
.BI \-\-file " FILE.wav"
it uses that recording instead of recording a new one.
Run it once; the correction is kept in
.IR ~/.config/gsp-keystudio/spl_offset .
.TP
.BI \-\-device " DEVICE"
Use this ALSA device (for example
.IR plughw:1,0 )
for recording or
.B \-\-play
instead of finding the ReSpeaker sound card automatically.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help message and exit.
.SH THE ANALYSIS REPORT
Each single-tap (or
.BR \-\-now )
recording is analyzed 200 times per second: every 5 ms the program takes a
2048-sample FFT (about 43 ms of sound at 48000 Hz) and measures that
moment's loudness and main frequency.
That is what lets it follow a sound whose pitch or loudness changes, such
as a frequency sweep.
.SS Volume
Volumes are in dB(A): the same A-weighted decibels a sound level meter or
phone meter app shows, where about 30 dB is a quiet room, 60 dB normal
conversation and 85 dB very loud.
The
.B Accuracy
line says how the microphone signal was turned into real decibels:
.TP
.B estimated from mic gain ...
Not calibrated yet.
The program reads the sound chip's current gain settings (input volume,
mic boost and digital volume, with
.BR amixer )
and combines them with the typical sensitivity of this kind of microphone.
The exact mic part on the HAT isn't published, so this is usually within
about 5 dB.
If the settings can't be read (for example when analyzing a file on
another computer), the installer's default settings are assumed.
.TP
.B calibrated
After
.BR \-\-calibrate :
the readings match the sound level meter you calibrated against.
Changing the capture volume or mic boost in
.B alsamixer
afterwards makes the calibration wrong; calibrate again.
.TP
.B Average (whole recording)
The energy average of the entire recording (what sound meters call Leq).
.TP
.B Lowest (quietest 1/8 s)
The quietest stretch, measured like a sound meter's "fast" setting
(1/8 second averages), and when it happened.
In a recording with pauses this is the room's background noise.
.TP
.B Highest (loudest 1/8 s)
The loudest stretch, and when it happened.
.TP
.B Rating
From the average:
SILENT (below 35 dB),
VERY QUIET (35\-50),
QUIET (50\-60),
MODERATE (60\-70),
LOUD (70\-80),
VERY LOUD (80\-90),
EXTREME (90 dB and above),
plus a
.B LOW [####\-\-\-\-] HIGH
meter from 20 to 110 dB.
.TP
.B WARNING: ... clipping
Shown when the sound was too loud for the microphones; the readings are
then lower than the real level.
.SS Frequency
The heading shows the analysis rate, FFT size and sample rate.
Before the frequency analysis, everything below about 20 Hz is filtered
out: real microphones pick up slow pressure changes and rumble that are
much stronger than speech and would otherwise drown out the lowest
frequencies.
No frequency below 20 Hz is ever reported.
.TP
.B Lowest freq / Highest freq
The lowest and highest main frequency heard, and when.
Every 5 ms frame contributes its strongest frequency, if that frame is
loud (within 30 dB of the loudest moment), clearly tonal (its peak at
least 10 dB above the rest of the spectrum) and steady (not straddling a
sudden start or stop).
For a sweep these are its start and end frequencies.
The first and last 21 ms of the recording can't be measured (half a
2048-sample window), so a sweep that starts right at 0 s reads slightly
inside its true range.
.TP
.B Dominant freq
The strongest frequency over the whole recording (the first of the top
peaks), accurate to about 1 Hz for a steady tone.
.TP
.B Spectral center
The "center of mass" of the sound; higher means a brighter sound.
.TP
.B Top peaks
The three strongest frequencies, at least 50 Hz apart, with each one's
strength relative to the strongest (+0.0 dB, the dominant frequency).
.TP
.B Band energy
Percent of the sound's energy in Low (20\-250 Hz), Mid (250\-4000 Hz) and
High (4000 Hz up to half the sample rate).
.PP
If the recording is too quiet (its loudest moment is below \-75 dB on the
mics' own scale), the frequency part is skipped.
.SH LEDS
The three LEDs on the HAT show what the program is doing:
.TP
.B Red countdown
Recording 3 seconds: all three LEDs light red, then one turns off each
second (3, 2, 1), so you can see how much time is left.
.TP
.B Blue
Analyzing.
.TP
.B Green to red
The result's loudness (green is quiet, red is loud).
.TP
.B Magenta
A session recording is in progress.
.TP
.B Off
The session has been saved, or the program has quit.
.SH PLAYING BACK RECORDINGS
Plug headphones or a speaker into the HAT's headphone jack, then:
.PP
.nf
.RS
gsp-keystudio \-\-play                  # play the newest recording
ls \-lt ~/recordings/                   # list them, newest first
gsp-keystudio \-\-play session_20260928_033906_241.wav
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B gsp-keystudio \-\-play
always uses the HAT.
.PP
You can also play files with
.BR aplay .
The installer makes the HAT the default sound device for your user (see
.I ~/.asoundrc
under
.BR FILES ),
so
.B aplay
plays through the HAT without any extra options.
To play through the Pi's own audio output instead, name its card, for
example
.BR "aplay \-D plughw:Headphones FILE.wav" .
Run
.B aplay \-l
to list the sound cards.
.PP
To listen on another computer, run this on that computer to copy the
files over, then open them in any media player:
.PP
.nf
.RS
scp pranav@<pi-address>:recordings/*.wav .
.RE
.fi
.PP
To analyze a saved recording again:
.PP
.nf
.RS
gsp-keystudio \-\-file ~/recordings/rec_20260928_033900_838.wav
.RE
.fi
.SH INSTALLATION
From the project folder, run once:
.PP
.nf
.RS
cd ~/GSP-2026
sudo ./install.sh  # say yes to "Reboot now?" the first time
.RE
.fi
.PP
The installer does the following:
.IP \(bu 2
Installs the needed packages (alsa-utils, numpy, the GPIO and SPI
libraries, man-db).
It works on a fresh Raspberry Pi OS Lite.
.IP \(bu 2
Turns on I2C and SPI.
.IP \(bu 2
Sets up the HAT's sound card with the kernel's built-in WM8960 driver
.RB ( dtoverlay=wm8960\-soundcard ),
removes the old seeed-voicecard driver if present, and turns on the
headphone output and microphones once the card appears.
.B \-\-no\-driver
skips this step.
.IP \(bu 2
Adds your user to the audio, gpio, spi and i2c groups.
.IP \(bu 2
Creates the recordings folder and the results file
.I analysis_results.txt
in it (an existing results file is kept).
.IP \(bu 2
Makes the HAT the default sound device for your user, so sound plays
through the HAT's headphone jack.
.IP \(bu 2
Installs this command and this manual page.
.PP
To update after
.BR "git pull" ,
run
.B sudo ./install.sh
again.
The installer can safely be run more than once, and existing recordings
are kept.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /home/pranav/recordings/
The recordings folder, created by the installer.
.RS
.TP
.I rec_YYYYmmdd_HHMMSS_mmm.wav
3-second recordings that were analyzed.
.TP
.I session_YYYYmmdd_HHMMSS_mmm.wav
Double-tap recordings.
.TP
.I analysis_results.txt
Created by the installer.
Every analysis is added to the end of it, under a
.B ###
line with the date, time and the analyzed file.
View it with
.BR "less ~/recordings/analysis_results.txt" .
.RE
.TP
.I ~/.asoundrc
Written by the installer: makes the HAT the default device for playing
and recording.
An existing file is first saved as
.IR ~/.asoundrc.bak\-gsp .
Delete it to go back to the Pi's own audio output.
.TP
.I ~/.config/gsp-keystudio/spl_offset
The volume correction saved by
.BR \-\-calibrate .
Delete it to go back to estimated volumes.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/gsp-keystudio
This command.
It sets
.B GSP_RECORDINGS_DIR
and
.B GSP_RESULTS_FILE
and runs the program.
.TP
.I /opt/gsp-keystudio/respeaker_analyzer.py
The program itself.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/man/man1/gsp-keystudio.1
This manual page.
.TP
.I /usr/local/sbin/gsp-audio-levels
One-time helper (run by
.BR gsp\-audio\-levels.service )
that turns on the HAT's headphone output and microphones.
Its log is
.IR /var/log/gsp-audio-levels.log .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GSP_RECORDINGS_DIR
Folder to save recordings in.
The
.B gsp-keystudio
command sets it to the folder the installer created; if it is unset,
.I /home/pranav/recordings
is used.
.TP
.B GSP_RESULTS_FILE
File that every analysis is added to.
The
.B gsp-keystudio
command sets it to the file the installer created; if it is unset,
.I analysis_results.txt
in the recordings folder is used.
.TP
.B GSP_CALIBRATION_FILE
Where
.B \-\-calibrate
saves and reads the volume correction (default
.IR ~/.config/gsp-keystudio/spl_offset ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including quitting with Ctrl+C.
.TP
.B 1
An error, printed as
.IR "Error: ..." ,
such as a missing file or a failed recording.
.TP
.B 2
An unknown or invalid option.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.TP
.B Nothing happens when I tap the button
Log out and back in (or reboot) once after installing, so the new gpio
group membership takes effect.
.TP
.B "No sound card / arecord failed"
Run
.BR "arecord \-l" ;
the list should show a seeed or wm8960 card.
If it doesn't, reboot, or run
.B sudo ./install.sh
again.
.TP
.B Recordings are silent, or playback is silent
Check
.I /var/log/gsp-audio-levels.log
and adjust the levels with
.B alsamixer
(press F6 to pick the seeed2micvoicec card; F3 for playback, F4 for
capture), then save them with
.BR "sudo alsactl store" .
.TP
.B "Playback fails with \(lqwrite error: Input/output error\(rq"
The old seeed-voicecard driver does not start the audio clock on newer
kernels
.RB ( dmesg
shows
.IR "no PCM clock" ).
Run
.B sudo ./install.sh
again from the project folder and reboot; it switches to the kernel's
built-in WM8960 driver.
.TP
.B "Sound still comes out of the Pi, not the HAT"
Run
.BR "aplay \-l" ;
it should list
.IR seeed2micvoicec .
If it doesn't, reboot once after installing.
Sound played with
.B sudo
uses root's settings, so play files without
.BR sudo .
.TP
.B "The installer stops with \(lqSome index files failed to download\(rq"
A package server did not respond.
Run
.B sudo apt\-get update
again, then re-run the installer.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B gsp-keystudio
Wait for button taps.
.TP
.B gsp-keystudio \-\-now
Record and analyze 3 seconds right away.
.TP
.B gsp-keystudio \-\-file ~/recordings/rec_20260928_033900_838.wav
Analyze a saved recording.
.TP
.B gsp-keystudio \-\-play
Play the newest recording through the HAT.
.TP
.B aplay ~/recordings/session_20260928_033906_241.wav
Play a recording with aplay (through the HAT by default).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR arecord (1),
.BR aplay (1),
.BR alsamixer (1)
